description:
Create nginx container for TLS termination.

**Files to create:**

### 1. apps/run.cms/nginx/Dockerfile.nginx
```dockerfile
FROM nginx:latest

# Copy self-signed certs for ALB->container TLS
COPY certs/nginx-selfsigned.crt /etc/nginx/ssl/nginx-selfsigned.crt
COPY certs/nginx-selfsigned.key /etc/nginx/ssl/nginx-selfsigned.key

# Copy nginx configuration
COPY nginx.conf /etc/nginx/nginx.conf

# Create html directory and copy placeholder
RUN mkdir -p /etc/nginx/html
COPY index.html /etc/nginx/html/index.html

EXPOSE 443

HEALTHCHECK --interval=5m --timeout=3s \
  CMD curl -k -f https://localhost/hello || exit 1

ENV NGINX_ENTRYPOINT_QUIET_LOGS=1

CMD ["nginx", "-g", "daemon off;"]
```

### 2. apps/run.cms/nginx/nginx.conf
```nginx
events {
  worker_connections 1024;
}

http {
  server_tokens off;

  real_ip_header X-Forwarded-For;
  real_ip_recursive on;
  set_real_ip_from 0.0.0.0/0;

  access_log /dev/stdout;
  error_log /dev/stderr warn;

  include       mime.types;
  default_type  application/octet-stream;
  sendfile      on;
  keepalive_timeout 65;

  # Strapi upstream (port 1337, NOT 3000)
  upstream strapi {
    server localhost:1337;
  }

  server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name localhost;

    ssl_certificate /etc/nginx/ssl/nginx-selfsigned.crt;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/nginx/ssl/nginx-selfsigned.key;

    # Health check endpoint
    location /hello {
      default_type "text/html";
      add_header x-health-check alive-cms-0001;
      return 200 '<html><body><h1>CMS OK</h1></body></html>';
    }

    # Proxy to Strapi
    location / {
      proxy_pass http://strapi;
      proxy_http_version 1.1;
      proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
      proxy_set_header Connection 'upgrade';
      proxy_set_header Host $host;
      proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
      proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto https;
      proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
      proxy_cache_bypass $http_upgrade;

      # Strapi can have large uploads
      client_max_body_size 100M;
    }
  }
}
```

### 3. apps/run.cms/nginx/index.html
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html><head><title>CMS</title></head>
<body><h1>Strapi CMS</h1></body></html>
```

### 4. apps/run.cms/nginx/certs/ (generate or copy)
```bash
# Option A: Copy from run.auth
cp -r apps/run.auth/nginx/certs apps/run.cms/nginx/

# Option B: Generate new
cd apps/run.cms/nginx/certs
openssl req -x509 -nodes -days 365 -newkey rsa:2048 \
  -keyout nginx-selfsigned.key \
  -out nginx-selfsigned.crt \
  -subj '/CN=localhost'
```

**Key difference from run.auth:**
- Upstream is `localhost:1337` (Strapi) not `localhost:3000` (Next.js)
- Larger client_max_body_size for media uploads

**Acceptance Criteria:**
- [ ] Dockerfile.nginx builds successfully
- [ ] nginx.conf proxies to port 1337
- [ ] Self-signed certs present in certs/
- [ ] Health check returns 200 on /hello

Define cms_storage in service.hcl using the user_uploads pattern from run-human.

IMPORTANT: CMS needs S3 buckets for:
1. Litestream SQLite replication (single region, accessed by all)
2. Media uploads (multi-region with replication)

Add to service.hcl:
```hcl
# S3 buckets for CMS
user_uploads = [
  # Litestream bucket - SQLite WAL replication
  {
    name         = "cms-litestream"
    service_name = "cms"
    regions      = ["us-east-1"]  # Single region - workers pull directly

    lifecycle = {
      uploads_expire_days   = 0  # Never expire
      processed_expire_days = 0
      enable_versioning     = true  # Required for point-in-time recovery
    }

    replication = {
      enabled = false  # Workers read from same bucket
    }
  },
  # Media bucket - uploaded assets
  {
    name         = "cms-media"
    service_name = "cms"
    regions      = ["us-east-1", "ca-central-1"]

    lifecycle = {
      uploads_expire_days   = 0  # Never expire media
      processed_expire_days = 0
      enable_versioning     = true
    }

    replication = {
      enabled = true
      replica_regions = [
        { label = "use1", full = "us-east-1" },
        { label = "cac1", full = "ca-central-1" }
      ]
    }
  }
]
```

Bucket names will be: uploads-dc34-cms-litestream-use1-{suffix} and uploads-dc34-cms-media-{region}-{suffix}

Reference: infra/terraform/live/site/services/run-human/service.hcl lines 326-347.

Install and configure AWS S3 upload provider for Strapi v5.

**Reference:** https://github.com/whereiskurt/defcon.run.33/blob/main/apps/strapi/site-tld/config/plugins.ts

**Steps:**
```bash
cd apps/run.cms/app
npm install @strapi/provider-upload-aws-s3@^5
```

**Configure in config/plugins.ts (Strapi v5 format):**
```typescript
export default ({ env }) => ({
  upload: {
    config: {
      provider: 'aws-s3',
      providerOptions: {
        baseUrl: env('CF_CDN_URL'),
        rootPath: env('CF_ROOT_PATH'),
        s3Options: {
          credentials: {
            accessKeyId: env('CF_ACCESS_KEY'),
            secretAccessKey: env('CF_SECRET_KEY'),
          },
          region: env('AWS_REGION'),
          params: {
            ACL: null,  // Required for buckets with ACLs disabled
            signedUrlExpires: env.int('AWS_SIGNED_URL_EXPIRES', 15 * 60),
            Bucket: env('CF_BUCKET_NAME'),
          },
        },
      },
      actionOptions: {
        upload: {},
        uploadStream: {},
        delete: {},
      },
    },
  },
});
```

**Environment variables needed (add to ECS task definition):**
- CF_CDN_URL: CloudFront URL (e.g., https://cms.defcon.run)
- CF_ROOT_PATH: Root path in bucket (e.g., uploads)
- CF_ACCESS_KEY: From SSM /dc34/uploads/{region}/cms-media/access_key_id
- CF_SECRET_KEY: From SSM /dc34/uploads/{region}/cms-media/secret_access_key
- CF_BUCKET_NAME: From SSM /dc34/uploads/{region}/cms-media/bucket_name
- AWS_REGION: {{REGION}}

**Key differences from v4:**
- Uses `s3Options` wrapper for credentials
- `ACL: null` required for modern S3 buckets
- signedUrlExpires uses env.int() for number parsing

**Acceptance Criteria:**
- [ ] @strapi/provider-upload-aws-s3 ^5.x in package.json
- [ ] config/plugins.ts uses v5 syntax with s3Options
- [ ] ACL set to null (not 'public-read')
- [ ] Environment variables documented for task definition

Create middleware to validate 'cms' service claim in OIDC tokens (Strapi v5).

**Reference:** Strapi v5 middleware registration and TypeScript syntax.

**File:** apps/run.cms/app/src/middlewares/oidc-services-check.ts

```typescript
import type { Core } from '@strapi/strapi';

export default (config: unknown, { strapi }: { strapi: Core.Strapi }) => {
  return async (ctx: any, next: () => Promise<void>) => {
    // Only check admin routes
    if (\!ctx.request.url.startsWith('/admin')) {
      return next();
    }

    // Skip if no authenticated user
    if (\!ctx.state.user) {
      return next();
    }

    // Check services claim from OIDC token
    const services = ctx.state.user.services || [];

    if (\!Array.isArray(services) || \!services.includes('cms')) {
      strapi.log.warn(
        `User ${ctx.state.user.email} denied CMS access - missing 'cms' service claim`
      );
      return ctx.forbidden(
        'You do not have permission to access the CMS. Contact an administrator to request access.'
      );
    }

    await next();
  };
};
```

**Register in config/middlewares.ts:**
```typescript
export default [
  'strapi::logger',
  'strapi::errors',
  {
    name: 'strapi::security',
    config: {
      // ... CSP config
    },
  },
  'strapi::cors',
  'strapi::query',
  'strapi::body',
  'strapi::session',
  'strapi::favicon',
  'strapi::public',
  // Custom middleware - add after authentication
  'global::oidc-services-check',
];
```

**Strapi v5 middleware changes:**
- Uses TypeScript
- Imports Core types from @strapi/strapi
- Same registration pattern in config/middlewares.ts

This ensures only users with 'services: ["cms", ...]' claim can access /admin/*.

**Acceptance Criteria:**
- [ ] Middleware file created at src/middlewares/oidc-services-check.ts
- [ ] Middleware registered in config/middlewares.ts
- [ ] Middleware checks for 'cms' in services array
- [ ] Users without 'cms' claim get 403 Forbidden
- [ ] TypeScript compiles without errors

Configure Strapi v5 OIDC SSO provider for admin panel authentication.

**Reference:** Strapi v5 has improved SSO support via the Enterprise Edition or community plugins.

**Option 1: Strapi Enterprise SSO (if available)**
Strapi Enterprise includes built-in SSO support. Configure in config/admin.ts:
```typescript
export default ({ env }) => ({
  auth: {
    secret: env('ADMIN_JWT_SECRET'),
    providers: [
      {
        uid: 'oidc',
        displayName: 'DEF CON Auth',
        icon: 'https://auth.defcon.run/icon.png',
        createStrategy: (strapi) => {
          // OIDC strategy configuration
          return new OIDCStrategy({
            issuer: 'https://auth.defcon.run',
            clientID: env('STRAPI_OIDC_CLIENT_ID'),
            clientSecret: env('STRAPI_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET'),
            callbackURL: 'https://cms.defcon.run/admin/connect/oidc/callback',
            scope: 'openid profile email',
          });
        },
      },
    ],
  },
  apiToken: {
    salt: env('API_TOKEN_SALT'),
  },
  transfer: {
    token: {
      salt: env('TRANSFER_TOKEN_SALT'),
    },
  },
});
```

**Option 2: Community Plugin (strapi-plugin-sso)**
If not using Enterprise, install community SSO plugin:
```bash
npm install strapi-plugin-sso
```

**Option 3: Custom OIDC Middleware**
Create custom authentication flow:
- Redirect unauthenticated admin requests to auth.defcon.run
- Handle callback and create/update Strapi admin user
- Validate services claim includes 'cms'

**Environment variables:**
- STRAPI_OIDC_CLIENT_ID: From SSM /dc34/secrets/{region}/strapi/oidc_client_id
- STRAPI_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET: From SSM /dc34/secrets/{region}/strapi/oidc_client_secret
- ADMIN_JWT_SECRET: From SSM /dc34/secrets/{region}/strapi/admin_jwt_secret

**NOTE:** The exact implementation depends on Strapi license and plugin availability. 
Research current options when implementing:
1. Check if Strapi Enterprise SSO is available
2. Evaluate community plugins (strapi-plugin-sso, etc.)
3. Consider custom middleware if needed

**Acceptance Criteria:**
- [ ] SSO method selected and documented
- [ ] OIDC client credentials configured
- [ ] Admin users can authenticate via auth.defcon.run
- [ ] Services claim validation in place (only 'cms' users can access)

Add ECR repository definitions to service.hcl for CMS containers.

**Add to service.hcl:**
```hcl
locals {
  versions = {
    nginx = trimspace(file("${get_terragrunt_dir()}/VERSION.nginx"))
    app   = trimspace(file("${get_terragrunt_dir()}/VERSION.app"))
  }

  # ECR repositories for CMS service
  ecr_repositories = [
    {
      name                 = "cms-nginx"
      regions              = ["us-east-1", "ca-central-1"]
      image_tag_mutability = "IMMUTABLE"
      lifecycle_policy = {
        max_image_count = 10
        expire_days     = 30
      }
    },
    {
      name                 = "cms-app"
      regions              = ["us-east-1", "ca-central-1"]
      image_tag_mutability = "IMMUTABLE"
      lifecycle_policy = {
        max_image_count = 10
        expire_days     = 30
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

**IMPORTANT naming:**
- Use `cms-nginx` and `cms-app` (NOT cms-webapp)
- Matches existing pattern: auth-nginx/auth-app, run-human-nginx/run-human-app

**Full ECR names after module processing:**
- dc34-cms-nginx (in both regions)
- dc34-cms-app (in both regions)

**Reference:** infra/terraform/live/site/services/auth/service.hcl lines 8-27

**Acceptance Criteria:**
- [ ] service.hcl parses: `terragrunt hclfmt`
- [ ] ECR names follow pattern: cms-nginx, cms-app
- [ ] Both regions specified
- [ ] IMMUTABLE tag mutability set
